{
  "dom_id": "step-e5677",
  "name": "Example: Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten",
  "problem": "There are $10$ employees in a company, and $3$ of them are to be selected to form a committee. How many different selections are possible?",
  "worked_solution": "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ employees, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those employees to form a committee, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to form the committee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{n}{C}_{r} & ={}_{10}{C}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{720}{6} \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
